use std
into dfm.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with df_ is exported). It checks a part for CNC milling and sheet-metal manufacturability: how deep a standard end mill must reach into pockets and slots from the six axis directions, sharp and tight inside corners, thin walls, deep holes, and sheet bend rules. Every length is in millimeters. A direction index d from 0 to 5 means looking along axis d div 2 (0 x, 1 y, 2 z) from the plus side when d is even and from the minus side when d is odd; the height of a point for direction d is the sign (plus 1 for even d, minus 1 for odd d) times its coordinate on that axis, so the tool comes down from large heights.

```amni
extern fn df_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "df_view_f64";
extern fn df_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "df_view_i32";
let TR real[10] = [0.396875, 0.79375, 1.190625, 1.5875, 2.38125, 3.175, 4.7625, 6.35, 9.525, 12.7];
```

`TR` holds the radii of the standard end mills from 1/32 in to 1 in diameter, smallest first.

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NV`, `NT`, `NH`, `NE`, `NB` (vertices, triangles, holes, corner entries, bends), `NX`, `NY` (grid cells across and down), `GA` (axis of the current direction), `GD` (current direction index), `NL` (height clusters), `WX`, `WY`, `I0`, `J0` (current window size and corner). Reals, all starting at 0.0: `GS` (sign of the current direction), `HH` (cell size), `OU`, `OV` (grid origin on the two other axes), `TOP` (highest height in the grid), `BOT` (lowest vertex height). Growable f64 arrays: `V` (3 per vertex), `TH`, `TC`, `AR` (1 per triangle), `C` (3 per triangle), `HL` (8 per hole), `EG` (12 per corner entry), `BD` (12 per bend), `PR` (12 parameters), `OUT` and `CR` (12 per finding), `LO`, `HI` (3 each), `HF`, `WK`, `MA`, `DP`, `RT`, `WD` (1 per grid cell), `LH` (1 per cluster), `E1`, `E2`, `EP` (1 per window cell), `FZ`, `FB`, `FD` (scratch lines). Growable i32 arrays: `T`, `TT` (3 per triangle), `TK`, `FL`, `CL`, `MK` (1 per grid cell), `ST`, `OD` (scratch index lists), `EV` (1 per corner entry), `VS` (1 per triangle), `FV` (scratch line), `DQ` (deque). `MK` holds bit flags per cell: 1 outside air, 2 through opening, 4 drilled hole, 8 corner leftover, 16 visited.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `rs(xs, n)` and `ri(xs, n)` take an f64 or i32 growable array by reference: xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n). Every array in this file is sized only through these four helpers; no block defines other sizing helpers.

A finding is 12 reals: code, severity (0 info, 1 warning, 2 error), value, limit, the point x, y and z, the direction index (minus 1 for none), three extra numbers a, b and c, and a sort key. Codes: 1 pocket too deep for the cutter that fits, 2 gap narrower than the smallest cutter, 3 sharp inside corner, 4 tight inside corner radius, 5 thin wall, 6 deep hole, 7 tight bend radius, 8 short flange, 9 hole too close to a bend, 10 sheet hole smaller than the thickness.

The exported function `df_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `V`, 3 `TH`, 4 `TC`, 5 `C`, 6 `AR`, 8 `HL`, 9 `EG`, 10 `BD`, 11 `PR`, 12 `OUT`, and i32 views 2 `T` and 7 `TT`, through df_view_f64 and df_view_i32.

The exported function `df_load(nv, nt, nh, ne, nb)` takes five i32 counts, stores them in `NV`, `NT`, `NH`, `NE` and `NB`, sizes with `zf` and `zi`: `V` to 3·nv zeros, `T`, `TT` and `C` to 3·nt zeros, `TH`, `TC` and `AR` to nt zeros, `HL` to 8·nh zeros, `EG` to 12·ne zeros, `EV` to ne zeros, `BD` to 12·nb zeros and `PR` to 12 zeros, gives `OUT` and `CR` `rs` of 12 times 128, and calls `df_views`.

`add(code, sev, value, limit, x, y, z, dir, a, b, c, key)` appends those 12 reals to `CR` in that order (code, sev and dir are ints stored as reals).

`bounds()` sizes `LO` and `HI` with `zf` to 3 zeros and sets them to the smallest and largest vertex coordinate on each axis, over all `NV` vertices of `V`.

`hof(x, y, z)` is the height of a point for the current direction: `GS` times its coordinate on axis `GA`. `uof(x, y, z)` and `vof(x, y, z)` are its coordinates on axes (`GA` plus 1) mod 3 and (`GA` plus 2) mod 3. `cellof(x, y, z)` is the index j times `NX` plus i of the grid cell holding the point, where i is the floor of (uof minus `OU`) divided by `HH` and j the floor of (vof minus `OV`) divided by `HH`, or minus 1 when i or j falls outside the grid.

`setgrid(d)` prepares direction d: `GD` is d, `GA` is d div 2, `GS` is 1.0 for even d and minus 1.0 otherwise. With u the axis (`GA` plus 1) mod 3 and v the axis (`GA` plus 2) mod 3, n is `PR`[4] or 256 when that is not above 0, s is the larger span of the two axes (`HI` minus `LO`), pad is the smaller of `TR`[9] and half of s, and `HH` is the larger of 0.0001 and s plus twice pad, all divided by n. pc is the ceiling of pad over `HH`, plus 3. `NX` is the ceiling of the u span over `HH` plus twice pc plus 1, and `NY` likewise for v; `OU` is `LO`[u] minus pc cells and `OV` is `LO`[v] minus pc cells, so a cutter as large as pad can sit beside the part. `BOT` is the smallest hof over all vertices. With `zf` and `zi`, `HF` becomes `NX`·`NY` cells of minus 1e30; `TK` and `CL` become that many minus 1; `FL` and `MK` that many 0; `DP`, `RT`, `WD` and `WK` that many 0.0; `ST` and `OD` get `ri` of `NX`·`NY`.

`raster()` fills `HF` with the highest surface height over each cell center. For each triangle t with corners `T`[3t], `T`[3t+1] and `T`[3t+2], it takes each corner's uof, vof and hof (ua, va, ha and so on) and the doubled signed area s equal to (ub minus ua)·(vc minus va) minus (uc minus ua)·(vb minus va); it skips the triangle when the absolute value of s is at most 1e-12 times `HH` squared. For each cell i from the larger of 0 and the floor of ((smallest u minus `OU`) over `HH` minus 0.5) up to the smaller of `NX` minus 1 and the ceiling of ((largest u minus `OU`) over `HH` minus 0.5), and j the same way on v with `OV` and `NY`, the center is px equal to `OU` plus (i plus 0.5)·`HH` and py equal to `OV` plus (j plus 0.5)·`HH`; l0 is ((ub minus px)·(vc minus py) minus (uc minus px)·(vb minus py)) over s, l1 is ((uc minus px)·(va minus py) minus (ua minus px)·(vc minus py)) over s and l2 is 1 minus l0 minus l1. When all three are at least minus 1e-7, the cell height becomes the larger of itself and l0·ha plus l1·hb plus l2·hc.

`enclose()` floods outside air (`ST` is only emptied, keeping its reserve): every cell on the grid border whose height is at most minus 1e29 is pushed on `ST` and marked with bit 1; while `ST` is not empty it pops a cell and pushes each of its four neighbors that is inside the grid, has height at most minus 1e29 and lacks bit 1, marking it with bit 1. Every remaining cell with height at most minus 1e29 is a through opening: its height becomes `BOT` and it gets bit 2. Then `TOP` is the largest height over all cells.

`drilled()` marks with bit 4 the cells inside holes drilled along the current axis: for each hole h whose direction component `HL`[8h plus 5 plus `GA`] has absolute value above 0.999, with center point (`HL`[8h+2], `HL`[8h+3], `HL`[8h+4]) and radius `HL`[8h] over 2 plus `HH`, every grid cell whose center lies within that radius of the center's uof and vof gets bit 4.

`seen()` records which corner probes the current direction can see. For each corner entry e, the probe is the point (`EG`[12e+3], `EG`[12e+4], `EG`[12e+5]) moved 2·`HH` along the direction (`EG`[12e+6], `EG`[12e+7], `EG`[12e+8]); with k the cellof the probe, the probe is seen when k is minus 1 or `HF`[k] is at most hof of the probe plus `HH`, and then `EV`[e] gets bit 1 shifted left by `GD`.

`edt1(n)` is the one-dimensional squared distance transform of Felzenszwalb and Huttenlocher on the first n entries of `FB` (0 at target samples, 1e20 elsewhere), written to `FD`, using `FV` (n ints) and `FZ` (n plus 1 reals) for the lower envelope of parabolas: v0 is 0, z0 is minus 1e30 and z1 is 1e30; each later q computes s as ((`FB`[q] plus q squared) minus (`FB`[v of k] plus v of k squared)) over (2q minus 2·v of k), stepping k back while s is at most z of k, then appends q with boundary s; the second pass gives each q the value (q minus v of k) squared plus `FB`[v of k] for the parabola k whose span holds q.

`edt()` replaces the `WX` by `WY` window `EP` (row-major, x fastest) by its squared distance transform in cells: first every column (copy into `FB`, `edt1` of `WY`, copy `FD` back) and then every row the same way with `WX`. `FB`, `FD` (with `zf`), `FV` (with `zi`) and `FZ` (with `zf`, one more) are sized for the longer side plus 1 first.

`levels()` groups floor cells by height, starting from `LH` given `rs` of 64 and `OD` emptied (keeping its reserve). A floor cell has height above minus 1e29 and below `TOP` minus `HH`. It puts the floor cells in `OD` sorted by height, highest first, and sets the step g to the larger of `HH` and (`TOP` minus `BOT`) over 40. Walking `OD` in order, a cell starts a new cluster when there is no cluster yet or its height is below the current cluster's first height minus g; the new cluster's representative height (appended to `LH`) is that cell's height. Each floor cell's `CL` is its cluster index, and `NL` ends as the number of clusters.

`cover(c)` finds, for every cell of cluster c, the largest standard cutter that can reach it. L is `LH`[c]. The window is the bounding box of the cluster's cells grown by the ceiling of 2·`TR`[9] over `HH` plus 2 cells and clipped to the grid; `I0`, `J0`, `WX` and `WY` describe it and `E1`, `E2` and `EP` are sized with `zf` to `WX`·`WY` zeros. A window cell is closed when its grid height is above L plus `HH`. `EP` is 0 at closed cells and 1e20 elsewhere; after `edt` it is copied into `E1`, the squared distance to material above the level. kp and kmin start at minus 1 and left counts the cluster cells. For k from 9 down to 0 while left is above 0: rp is `TR`[k] over `HH`; when rp is below 0.75 the loop stops; kmin becomes k; `EP` is 0 where `E1` is above rp squared (where a cutter of that radius fits) and 1e20 elsewhere, then `edt`. Every cluster cell in the window whose `TK` is still minus 1 and whose `EP` is at most rp squared plus 1e-9 gets `TK` equal to k and left drops by 1; when kp is at least 0 and the square root of its `E2` minus `TR`[kp] over `HH` is at most 0.5 times `TR`[kp] over `HH`, the cell also gets bit 8 (it is the corner left by the bigger cutter, judged by the corner rules instead). When any cell was assigned, `EP` is copied into `E2` and kp becomes k. After the loop each cluster cell still unassigned gets `WD` equal to 2 times the square root of its `E1` times `HH`; when kmin is 0 and it is not a corner leftover by the same test against kp (skip that test when kp is minus 1), its `FL` becomes 2.

`maxfilter(r)` sizes `MA` and `WK` with `zf` and `DQ` with `zi` to the longer grid side plus 1, then sets `WK` to the largest `HF` in the square of cells within r of each cell, clipped to the grid: first along rows into `MA` and then along columns from `MA` into `WK`, each with the classic monotonic-deque sliding-window maximum on `DQ`.

`flags()` judges tool reach. tz is 4. For each tool k from 0 to 9 that has at least one cell with `TK` equal to k, without bits 4 and 8, whose `TOP` minus height over 2·`TR`[k] is above tz: `maxfilter` with r equal to the ceiling of 3·`TR`[k] over `HH` plus 1, and then for each such cell depth is the smaller of `WK` and `TOP`, minus the cell height; a through cell (bit 2) is skipped for odd directions and its depth halves otherwise; ratio is depth over 2·`TR`[k]; when ratio is above tz the cell gets `FL` 1, `DP` depth and `RT` ratio. Then when any cell has `FL` 2, `maxfilter` with r equal to the ceiling of 3·`TR`[0] over `HH` plus 1, and each cell with `FL` 2 gets depth the same way (the smaller of `WK` and `TOP`, minus the cell height, halved for through cells); its `FL` returns to 0 when it has bit 4, when it is a through cell on an odd direction, or when the depth is at most `HH`, else `DP` becomes depth.

`components()` turns flagged cells into findings. For each cell with `FL` above 0 and without bit 16, it floods the four-connected cells with the same `FL`, marking bit 16 and counting n, and keeps the worst cell w: the largest `RT` for `FL` 1 and the largest `DP` for `FL` 2. Groups smaller than 3 cells are dropped. The point has coordinate `GS` times `HF`[w] on axis `GA`, `OU` plus (i plus 0.5)·`HH` on axis (`GA` plus 1) mod 3 and `OV` plus (j plus 0.5)·`HH` on axis (`GA` plus 2) mod 3. `FL` 1 adds code 1 with severity 2 when `RT` is above 10 and 1 otherwise, value `RT`, limit 10 for severity 2 and 4 otherwise, direction `GD`, a the cutter diameter 2·`TR`[`TK`[w]], b `DP`, c n·`HH` squared and key `RT`. `FL` 2 adds code 2 with severity 2, value `WD`, limit 2·`TR`[0], direction `GD`, a 0, b `DP`, c n·`HH` squared and key `DP`.

`corners()` judges the B-rep corner entries after all six directions. Entry e has kind `EG`[12e] (0 a sharp concave edge, 1 a concave fillet), radius `EG`[12e+1], length `EG`[12e+2], point `EG`[12e+3] to `EG`[12e+5], the axis index ax in `EG`[12e+9] and face-normal directions d1 and d2 in `EG`[12e+10] and `EG`[12e+11] (minus 1 when not axis aligned). Its open direction is 2·ax when `EV` has that bit, else 2·ax plus 1 when it has that bit, else minus 1. A sharp edge seen from d1 or d2 is fine (a flat cutter coming along that face normal makes it); otherwise it adds code 3, severity 2, value 0, limit 0, the open direction, a the length and key the length. A fillet with no open direction, or whose radius times 3 is at least its length, is skipped; otherwise ratio is length over twice the radius and it adds code 4 with severity 2 when ratio is above 10, 1 when above 4 and 0 otherwise, value the radius, limit length over 3, the open direction, a ratio, b length and key ratio.

`thin()` finds thin walls from the per-triangle thickness. w is `PR`[1] and e is `PR`[2]. A triangle is thin when its `TH` is above 0, below w and below 1e29 and its `TC` is below minus 0.5. `VS` becomes `NT` zeros with `zi` and `ST` gets `ri` of `NT`. Each unvisited thin triangle starts a group flooded through `TT` neighbors (minus 1 means none) that are thin, summing `AR` and keeping the triangle with the smallest `TH`. Groups whose area is below 0.5 times w squared are dropped; the rest add code 5 with severity 2 when the smallest thickness is below e and 1 otherwise, value that thickness, limit w, the centroid `C` of that triangle, direction minus 1, a the area, b e, and key minus the thickness.

`deep()` checks hole depth. For each hole h with diameter `HL`[8h] above 0 and depth `HL`[8h+1], ratio is depth over diameter; above 40 it adds severity 2 with limit 40 and above 10 severity 1 with limit 10, code 6, value ratio, the point (`HL`[8h+2], `HL`[8h+3], `HL`[8h+4]) moved half the depth along (`HL`[8h+5], `HL`[8h+6], `HL`[8h+7]), direction minus 1, a the diameter, b the depth and key ratio.

`sheet()` checks bent sheet metal with thickness t equal to `PR`[3]. Bend b has inside radius `BD`[12b], shortest flat flange `BD`[12b+1] (minus 1 when unknown), an axis point `BD`[12b+2] to `BD`[12b+4] at the middle of the bend, the unit axis `BD`[12b+5] to `BD`[12b+7], length `BD`[12b+8] and a surface point `BD`[12b+9] to `BD`[12b+11]. An inside radius below t adds code 7, severity 1, value the radius, limit t, at the surface point, key minus the radius. When the flat flange is at least 0 and it plus the radius plus t is below 4t, it adds code 8, severity 1, value that flange length, limit 4t, at the surface point, key minus the length. For each hole h with center p equal to its point moved half its depth along its direction: when `PR`[5] is above 0 and the diameter is below t it adds code 10, severity 1, value the diameter, limit t, at p, key minus the diameter. For each bend whose axis is nearly perpendicular to the hole direction (absolute dot product below 0.1), along is the dot product of p minus the axis point with the axis; when its absolute value is at most half the bend length plus the hole diameter, q is the length of (p minus the axis point) minus the axis times along, m is the bend radius plus half of t, and when q is above m, s is the square root of q squared minus m squared, minus half the diameter; when s is below 2t plus the radius it adds code 9, severity 1, value the larger of s and 0, limit 2t plus the radius, at p, a the diameter, key minus s.

`flush()` moves findings from `CR` to `OUT`, marking taken ones in a local i32 array sized with `zi`: for each code from 1 to 10 it repeatedly picks the not yet taken finding of that code with the highest severity and, among equal severities, the largest key, appending its 12 reals to `OUT`, at most 7 per code.

The exported function `df_run()` returns an i32. It gives `OUT` and `CR` `rs` of 12 times 128 and calls `bounds`. When `PR`[0] is 1 (milling) it calls `thin` when `PR`[1] is above 0, then `deep`, sizes `EV` to `NE` zeros with `zi`, and for each direction d from 0 to 5 calls `setgrid`, `raster`, `enclose` and `seen`, and when `TOP` minus `BOT` is at least 2·`HH` also `drilled`, `levels`, `cover` for each cluster, `flags` and `components`; after the six directions it calls `corners`. When `PR`[0] is 2 (sheet) it calls `sheet`. Then `flush`, `df_views`, and it returns the number of findings in `OUT` (its length over 12).
